  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 364,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 164,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 55844,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 193,
        "headingId": 164,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Lipomas may occur anywhere in the digestive system. The large intestine is the most commonly affected site, with most lipomas arising in the caecum and the ascending colon {22298986; 25368224}. Less often, lipomas can be seen in the small intestine, most commonly in the ileum, followed by the stomach and the oesophagus {28842049; 28852321; 27902957}. Lipomas typically arise in the submucosa; &lt;&nbsp;10% involve the subserosa. Intramucosal lipomas are rare, but they can rarely represent sentinel lesions for Cowden syndrome (<em>PTEN</em> hamartoma tumour syndrome) {26826408; 27661969; 29192650}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-04-15T16:18:17"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 365,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 165,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 55845,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 193,
        "headingId": 165,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Gastrointestinal lipomas are usually asymptomatic and often incidental findings during endoscopy, surgery, or autopsy. A variety of symptoms may occur when these tumours reach a large size, usually &gt;&nbsp;2&nbsp;cm for the small intestine and &gt;&nbsp;4&nbsp;cm for the stomach and colon {22298986; 25368224; 28852321}. The symptoms vary from mild abdominal pain and changes in bowel habits to life-threatening complications, because gastrointestinal lipomas may cause intestinal obstruction due to colocolonic intussusception, bleeding, and perforation {22298986; 25368224; 28852321}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-04-15T16:18:17"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 366,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 166,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 55846,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 193,
        "headingId": 166,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The prevalence of lipomas in the large bowel is estimated at 0.2&ndash;4.4% of patients undergoing endoscopy {25368224}. Peak incidence is within the sixth decade of life, with a possible female predilection {22298986}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-04-15T16:18:17"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 367,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 167,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 55847,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 193,
        "headingId": 167,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-04-15T16:18:17"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 368,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 168,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 55848,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 193,
        "headingId": 168,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>As many as 75% of lipomas harbour simple chromosomal aberrations. Abnormalities of 12q13-q14 are most common, usually with rearrangements of the <em>HMGA2</em> gene, encoding a member of the high-mobility group of proteins {7670494}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-04-15T16:18:17"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 375,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 207,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 55849,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 193,
        "headingId": 207,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Gastrointestinal lipomas vary considerably in size, from subcentimetre lesions to tumours measuring &gt;&nbsp;10&nbsp;cm. They may present as sessile or pedunculated masses.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-04-15T16:18:17"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 369,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 169,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 55850,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 193,
        "headingId": 169,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Microscopically, lipomas consist of mature adipocytes of uniform size without substantial cytological atypia or atypical hyperchromatic stromal cells. Ulceration of the overlying mucosa may be observed, especially in large lesions. In intramucosal lipomas, the adipocytes are seen in the basal portion of the lamina propria, often surrounding the crypts. Intramucosal lipomas must be distinguished from pseudolipomatosis, which is caused by the infiltration of gas into the mucosa during endoscopy. Rarely, subtypes described in soft tissues (e.g. angiolipoma) can occur in digestive system sites. Angiolipomas show prominent capillary vessels with scattered fibrin microthrombi.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-04-15T16:18:17"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 370,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
